  {
   "id": "2A2quF2KFgAJ",
   "from": "Phil Chalmers",
   "author": "b330c2028fed415b",
   "date": "2019-02-01T14:07:50Z",
   "subject": "Re: mirt solution depents on item order",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hugo is correct, these models are numerically equivalent to each other (you can use anova(mod1, mod2) to verify the likelihood information as well). Numeral optimizer often work slightly differently depending on the starting values (including ordering due to the computation of a quasi-Hessian in the quasi-Newton updates), so this is driven by the M-step updates. But, if you wan the solutions to be even more similar, just decrease the TOL input to something even smaller, like TOL=1e-8. <div><br clear=\"all\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Phil</div></div></div></div><br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Fri, Feb 1, 2019 at 6:07 AM Hugo Harada &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">hugo....@adaptativa.com.br</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">Hi Kornelius,</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\">I ran your code and I got similar results for both cases. Is that what you are seeing?</div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><br></div><div class=\"gmail_default\" style=\"font-size:small\"><div class=\"gmail_default\">data &lt;- expand.table(LSAT7)</div><div class=\"gmail_default\">mod1 &lt;- mirt(data,2)</div><div class=\"gmail_default\">mod2 &lt;- mirt(data[c(2,4,3,1,5)],2)</div><div class=\"gmail_default\">coef(mod1)</div><div class=\"gmail_default\">coef(mod2)</div><div class=\"gmail_default\">M2(mod1)</div><div class=\"gmail_default\">M2(mod2)</div><div><br></div><div><div><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/mirt-package/attach/168a5db8aa0dd8/image.png?part=0.1&amp;view=1\" alt=\"image.png\" width=\"678\" height=\"876\" style=\"margin-right:0px\"><br></div></div><div><br></div></div></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">Em sex, 1 de fev de 2019 às 07:52, Kor_Sch &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">korneliu...@gmail.com</a>&gt; escreveu:<br></div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">Sorry, I meant:<div><p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">data &lt;- expand.table(LSAT7)</p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">mod1 &lt;- mirt(data,2)</p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">mod2 &lt;- mirt(data[c(2,4,3,1,5)],2)</p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">The differences becomes obvious looking at the summary or coef output, or the M2 statistik.</p><br>Am Freitag, 1. Februar 2019 10:39:50 UTC+1 schrieb Kor_Sch:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0px 0px 0px 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">Hey Phil,</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">using your package I became aware of some odd behavior of\r\nthe mirt-function.</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">By coincidence I realized that the results change depending\r\non the order of items within the data set.</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">Here is a simple example of what I mean: </p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">data &lt;- expand.table(LSAT7)</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">mod1 &lt;- mirt(data,2)</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">mod2 &lt;- mirt(data[2,4,3,1,5],2)</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">However, mod1 and mod 2 are not equivalent. How can this be?</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">Regards,</p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">Kornelius</p></div></blockquote></div></div>\r\n\r\n<p></p>\r\n\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;mirt-package&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">mirt-package...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nFor more options, visit <a hr