   "date": "2020-06-09T01:52:42Z",
   "subject": "Re: mirt solution depents on item order",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi everyone, </div><div><br></div><div>I had the same issue, which surprised me. I am doing analysis using a multidimensional IRT model. I just realized that the item order changed the part of factor loadings, even after I decrease the TOL input to 1e-8. It makes me question my analysis results as they are very unstable.  I am wondering what might be the reason behind this unstable result? </div><div><br></div><div> Also, after I adjusted the TOL, models failed to converge. What might be the reason and how I could adjust for it? </div><div><br></div><div>My code is:</div><div><div>Call:</div><div>mirt(data = d2, model = 1, method = &quot;MHRM&quot;, calcNull = TRUE, </div><div>    TOL = 1e-08, verbose = F, technical = list(set.seed(12345)), </div><div>    QMC = T)</div><div><br></div><div>Full-information item factor analysis with 1 factor(s).</div><div>FAILED TO CONVERGE within 1e-08 tolerance after 2000 MHRM iterations.</div><div>mirt version: 1.32.1 </div><div>M-step optimizer: NR1 </div><div>Latent density type: Gaussian </div><div>Average MH acceptance ratio(s): 0.407 </div><div><br></div><div>Log-likelihood = NaN, SE = NaN</div><div>Estimated parameters: 34 </div><div>AIC = NaN; AICc = NaN</div><div>BIC = NaN; SABIC = NaN</div></div><div><br></div><div>Thank you!</div><div>Yuanjin</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br></div><br>On Friday, February 1, 2019 at 1:39:50 AM UTC-8, Kor_Sch wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">Hey Phil,</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">using your package I became aware of some odd behavior of\r\nthe mirt-function.</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">By coincidence I realized that the results change depending\r\non the order of items within the data set.</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">Here is a simple example of what I mean: </p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">data &lt;- expand.table(LSAT7)</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">mod1 &lt;- mirt(data,2)</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">mod2 &lt;- mirt(data[2,4,3,1,5],2)</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">However, mod1 and mod 2 are not equivalent. How can this be?</p>\r\n\r\n<p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">Regards,</p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" align=\"left\" style=\"line-height:normal\">Kornelius</p></div></blockquote></div>"
    ]
   ]
